# DevOps And Local Operations

## Environment And Secrets

- Use environment variables for all runtime configuration.
- Commit `.env.example` with placeholders only. Never commit `.env`, database dumps, backups, tokens, passwords, or real connection strings.
- Required settings include `DATABASE_URL`, `APP_SECRET_KEY`, `APP_BASE_URL`, `ADMIN_USERNAME`, `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE`, `MCP_ENABLED`, and `LOG_LEVEL`.
- If first-run setup supports `ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD`, it must be one-time use and never logged.
- Do not install missing host packages, CLIs, package-manager dependencies, or system tools from an agent session. If a required tool is missing, stop and ask the user to install or authenticate it manually, then continue after they confirm it is ready.
- Do not run host-level Playwright installers such as `playwright install --with-deps` outside Docker. Browser runtime dependencies belong in the app image or an existing app container.

## Docker Compose

- Keep compose files under `deployment/`.
- Use `deployment/docker-compose.local.yml` for app plus local PostgreSQL.
- Use `deployment/docker-compose.app.yml` for app-only deployment against an external PostgreSQL URL.
- Build FastAPI images from an official Python base image and use exec-form commands.
- Install Playwright browsers and their Linux runtime dependencies only inside the Docker image or app container. The Dockerfile may run `python -m playwright install --with-deps chromium`; Codex should not install those packages on the host.
- Do not run Docker with `sudo`.
- On the dev host, do not run Compose from a temporary worktree with the default project name if the live dev app uses the same compose file name/path convention. Use a unique `COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME` for preflight containers, or run checks inside the existing dev project intentionally.
- Preflight checks must not recreate, stop, or replace the user's live dev app container unless the user explicitly asks to restart dev.
- Before expensive Docker builds, check host capacity with `df -h / /tmp` and Docker usage with `docker system df`.
- After isolated preflight builds, failed builds, or Playwright/browser image rebuilds, clean up temporary images and unused build cache so browser layers do not fill the dev host. Prefer targeted cleanup such as `docker image rm <preflight-image>` and `docker builder prune`; use broad `docker system prune` only with explicit operator approval.
- Never remove Docker volumes or project containers that may contain tracker data as part of image cleanup unless the target project and volume are explicitly named and confirmed.

## Migrations And Startup

- Run `alembic upgrade head` as an explicit documented step before app startup in deployment.
- Local development may provide a helper script, but it must make migration execution visible.
- Do not auto-drop, recreate, or truncate databases from container startup.
- A deployment is not verified until `/health` confirms database connectivity.

## GitHub Actions Workflow Safety

- Workflow YAML must be ASCII-only.
- Validate YAML syntax before pushing when workflows change.
- Keep workflows small: lint/test, Docker build, and mirror rules are separate concerns.
- Do not author workflows directly on `main` unless explicitly requested.
- Use concurrency for deploy or mirror workflows that can race.

## UAT Deploy Source-Of-Truth Gate

- A request to "deploy to UAT" means the current dev checkout is expected to be committed, pushed, and deployed by exact Git SHA.
- Before triggering a UAT deploy workflow, check `git status --short`, the current branch, local `HEAD`, and the matching remote ref. If uncommitted product, UI, schema, deployment, or documentation changes exist, stop and either commit/push them or get explicit user confirmation to deploy a named older SHA.
- Pass the exact intended committed SHA as `expected_deploy_sha` when dispatching `.github/workflows/local-pipeline.yml`; the workflow must refuse to deploy if `GITHUB_SHA` differs.
- Do not describe UAT as matching dev unless both the code SHA and the intended data state are accounted for. GitHub Actions deploys committed code; it cannot include local uncommitted files.
- UAT database state is preserved by default. Normal UAT deploys must not reset the UAT PostgreSQL volume, drop/recreate the UAT database, or overwrite UAT data with dev data.
- Dev database state is separate from code deployment. Copying or restoring dev data into UAT requires a separate, explicit, backup-first data migration plan; never infer it from "deploy to UAT."
- Production database state is preserved by default. Production deploys must use forward-only migrations, take a pre-migration backup, and must not reset volumes, truncate tables, downgrade migrations, or restore data unless the user explicitly requests a production restore from a named backup.
- Production data may be copied down to overwrite UAT or dev for testing, but that is a separate data refresh workflow, not deployment. It must require explicit source/target naming, backup the target before overwrite, preserve production unchanged, sanitize or document secret/session handling, and verify the restored target with migrations, `/health`, smoke tests, and login/setup expectations.
- Never copy dev or UAT data upward into production.
- In closeout, report the deployed SHA and whether target data was preserved, freshly initialized, or explicitly restored/refreshed from another environment.

## Mirror Rules Workflow

- This project mirrors `.cursor/rules/` to `spacemanspiff99/vibecoding` after rules changes land on `main`.
- The vibecoding path is `projects/issue-tracker/cursor-rules/`.
- This repository is the authoritative source for issue-tracker rules and guidance. Vibecoding receives mirrored guidance only.
- The mirror is one-way from this project to vibecoding. Never edit mirrored rule content in vibecoding by hand.
- The GitHub secret must be named `VIBECODING_MIRROR_TOKEN` and scoped only to vibecoding contents read/write.

## Verification Commands

Use the relevant subset before completion:

```bash
df -h / /tmp
docker system df
python -m pytest tests/
alembic upgrade head
docker compose -f deployment/docker-compose.local.yml up --build
python -c "import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open('.github/workflows/mirror-rules.yml'))"
LC_ALL=C grep -RIn '[^ -~]' .cursor/rules .github/workflows documentation/standards
```

If a command cannot run because the project is not initialized yet, say so and verify the files that do exist.
